\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{xcolor}

\title{Per-Example Predictive Multiplicity in Hybrid Linear Recurrent Architectures}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Hybrid linear recurrent architectures that interleave attention with recurrence are increasingly deployed, yet how attention affects per-example behavior across backbones remains unquantified. Using the controlled model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}---three architectures, five attention ratios, two scales (340M/1.3B), single training runs per configuration---we evaluate 10 benchmarks and decompose per-example effects on four (LAMBADA, HellaSwag, PIQA, ARC-Easy). Per-example predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} varies across tasks ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$) while pairwise Jaccard of helped/hurt sets remains uniformly low ($\leq 0.10$); multiplicity magnitude depends on task type, not attention dose. Aggregate convergence, by contrast, is task-specific: only LAMBADA provides exploratory evidence for monotonic variance reduction (Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$), consistent with attention compensating for retrieval differences across recurrent backbones. On LAMBADA, aggregate variance decreases gradually while per-example overlap saturates at the first attention level---the two are decoupled.
\end{abstract}

\input{sections/introduction}

\input{sections/related_work}

\input{sections/method}

\input{sections/experiments}

\input{sections/conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

Our analysis has several limitations.

\paragraph{Coverage.} Per-example analysis covers four main benchmarks; BoolQ and WinoGrande are supplementary (Appendix~\ref{app:boolq}, \ref{app:winogrande}). The correlation between $\alpha$ and task accuracy is moderate but not significant ($\rho = -0.60$, $p = .24$); HellaSwag (60\%, $\alpha = 0.15$) and PIQA (73\%, $\alpha = 0.14$) show low $\alpha$ despite varied accuracy, while benchmarks near chance level (BoolQ, WinoGrande) show the highest $\alpha$. Accuracy alone does not explain the variation, though we cannot fully rule out more complex interactions. We examine three recurrent architectures from one model zoo; the controlled design (shared training data, tokenizer, hyperparameters) isolates architectural effects but limits generalization to other hybrid families.

\paragraph{Methodological constraints.} With only $n = 5$ attention ratios, the minimum achievable two-tailed $p$-value is approximately 0.017, contributing to the Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$ for LAMBADA not reaching conventional significance. Each zoo configuration corresponds to a single training run; we cannot fully disentangle architectural from seed-specific effects, though the $3\times$ Jaccard gap (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) provides proxy evidence for architecture as the dominant factor. For context, \citet{mccoy2020berts} report that BERT models from different fine-tuning seeds disagree on ${\sim}5$--$7$\% of MNLI examples; however, their metric (pairwise prediction agreement) differs from ours (Jaccard of transition sets when adding attention), so direct numerical comparison is not meaningful. A proper calibration would require multiple pretraining seeds per architecture within this zoo, which is not available; without this baseline, we cannot bound the seed contribution to our observed cross-architecture disagreement. The within-architecture Jaccard (${\sim}0.33$) compares models at different attention ratios, not different random seeds; it therefore provides a proxy for, not a direct measurement of, architecture-versus-seed effects. Consequently, all cross-architecture per-example claims are conditioned on single training runs per configuration. At 340M, $\alpha$ is systematically higher than at 1.3B (by up to 8.4pp on ARC-Easy), but pairwise Jaccard remains stable across scales ($\bar{J} = 0.08$--$0.10$), indicating that the qualitative pattern of per-example multiplicity is more robust than its absolute magnitude. Our attention weight analysis is descriptive, not causal; it compares aggregate distributions and does not resolve whether per-example attention behavior differs on the specific examples where architectures diverge. We compare only two scale points (340M, 1.3B)---a binary observation, not a scaling law.

\paragraph{Pre-training only.} All models are evaluated after pre-training without fine-tuning; per-example effects and the decoupling pattern may not persist after task-specific adaptation. We evaluate only discrete accuracy; continuous metrics such as log-probability or calibrated confidence may reveal different patterns of multiplicity.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work analyzes publicly available pretrained language models from the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. No new data collection, human subjects research, or model training is involved. We identify per-example behavioral differences across architectures, which may inform practitioners about the limitations of aggregate benchmark comparisons but does not introduce new risks.

\bibliography{references}

\appendix
\input{sections/appendix}

\end{document}

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We analyze how attention affects cross-architecture performance variance using models from the hybrid recurrent model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. Our analysis spans three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2), five attention ratios (0\% pure recurrent, 4\%, 7.7\%, 14.3\%, 25\%), and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). In the h$k$-1 notation used throughout, one attention layer is interleaved per every $k{+}1$ layers; h3-1 denotes 25\% attention. All models within each scale share identical training data and hyperparameters; they differ only in architecture and attention configuration. We evaluate on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed task categories, plus one supplementary benchmark (COPA, $n = 100$).

\subsection{Task-Specific Convergence}\label{ssec:task_convergence}

Table~\ref{tab:convergence} reports cross-architecture $\sigma$ at each attention ratio for all benchmarks at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA is the only benchmark exhibiting monotonic convergence: $\rho = -1.0$ ($p = .017$; BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0,\,-0.7]$, heuristic given $n = 3$ architectures, \S\ref{ssec:statistical_methods}; $\sigma$ drops 79\%; Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$; excluding GDN: $\rho = -0.7$, $p = .233$, \S\ref{ssec:robustness}). Statistical power is inherently limited with $n = 5$ ordinal levels, so this convergence is best interpreted as exploratory. This is consistent with attention compensating for differences in how recurrent architectures retrieve from context \citep{arora2024zoology}---LAMBADA requires predicting a final word from long-range dependencies, and attention provides direct token-level access that narrows architectural differences in recall capacity \citep{arora2024based}. The three recurrent backbones differ in how they compress sequential context---exponential decay (RetNet), gated expansion (HGRN2), and delta rules (GDN)---creating divergent retrieval capacities that attention bypasses via direct token access. For reasoning and comprehension tasks, the bottleneck lies in multi-step inference rather than context retrieval, so attention cannot similarly eliminate cross-architecture variance. No other benchmark shows significant convergence (all $|\rho| \leq 0.7$, $p \geq .233$; Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}). Full convergence discussion appears in Appendix~\ref{app:convergence_detail}. Scale analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:scale_convergence}) shows this convergence is present at 1.3B but absent at 340M.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_task_specificity.pdf}
\caption{Spearman $\rho$ between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ for each benchmark at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA ($\rho = -1.0$; BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0,\,-0.7]$, heuristic) is the only benchmark with a negative correlation whose confidence interval excludes zero. Benchmarks are grouped by task category.}
\label{fig:task_specificity}
\end{figure}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-architecture convergence across benchmarks (1.3B scale). $\rho$: Spearman correlation between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ across five configurations. $p$: exact permutation test (two-tailed, 120 permutations). $\sigma$ values reported $\times 10^{2}$ (accuracy percentage points). Ratio: $\sigma_{\text{pure}} / \sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$. \textbf{Bold}: only benchmark with $\rho = -1.0$.}
\label{tab:convergence}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
Benchmark & Category & $\rho$ & $p$ & $\sigma_{\text{pure}}$ & $\sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$ & Ratio \\
\midrule
\textbf{LAMBADA$^{\ddagger}$} & \textbf{Retrieval} & $\mathbf{-1.0}$ & \textbf{.017} & \textbf{2.41} & \textbf{0.49} & \textbf{4.87} \\
BoolQ & Comprehension & $0.2$ & .783 & 1.63 & 2.36 & 0.69 \\
ARC-C & Reasoning & $0.6$ & .350 & 0.96 & 1.32 & 0.72 \\
HellaSwag & Reasoning & $0.4$ & .517 & 1.17 & 1.22 & 0.96 \\
PIQA & Reasoning & $-0.5$ & .450 & 0.54 & 0.34 & 1.59 \\
WinoGrande & Reasoning & $0.1$ & .950 & 0.67 & 0.78 & 0.86 \\
ARC-E & Mixed & $-0.7$ & .233 & 1.00 & 0.13 & 7.70 \\
MMLU & Mixed & $-0.7$ & .233 & 0.92 & 0.41 & 2.27 \\
SciQ & Mixed & $0.1$ & .950 & 0.87 & 0.74 & 1.19 \\
XStoryCloze & Mixed & $-0.6$ & .350 & 0.82 & 0.55 & 1.49 \\
\midrule
COPA$^{\dagger}$ & Reasoning & $-0.67$ & .267 & 2.63 & 0.47 & 5.57 \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize $^{\dagger}$Supplementary ($n = 100$); excluded from multiple comparison correction.} \\
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize $^{\ddagger}$GDN h3-1 uses 16 attention heads; all other configurations use 32 (\S\ref{ssec:robustness}).} \\
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize Bonferroni-adjusted $p = 6 \times 2/120 = 0.100$ (exact); 0.102 arises from rounding raw $p$ to .017 before multiplying.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\subsection{Per-Example Divergence}\label{ssec:per_example}

Do architectures converge by improving on the same examples? We compare each architecture's pure-recurrent baseline against its h3-1 hybrid on 5{,}153 LAMBADA examples, classifying each as helped (correct only in hybrid), hurt (correct only in pure), or unchanged. Although the decomposition is binary, we verify below that it captures genuine separation in the continuous confidence space (Cohen's $d = 0.73$--$0.92$ between helped and hurt on LAMBADA; cross-benchmark details in Appendix~\ref{app:cm_cross}). This binarization discards confidence magnitude---a transition from 0.51 to 0.49 is treated identically to one from 0.99 to 0.01---so we supplement it with continuous confidence margin analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:confidence_margin}).

Each architecture displays a distinct profile of per-example changes (Table~\ref{tab:per_example}). GDN loses a net 153 examples (462 helped, 615 hurt), RetNet loses 69 (426/495), and HGRN2 gains 153 (548/395). HGRN2 is the only architecture for which attention produces a net per-example benefit. Pairwise Jaccard similarity is low: mean $\bar{J} \leq 0.104$ for helped, $\leq 0.096$ for hurt, indicating ${\sim}90$\% non-overlap. Overlap significantly exceeds the independence baseline ($J_{\text{null}} \approx 0.05$, all $z > 5.9$, $p < .0001$; Appendix~\ref{app:jaccard_permutation}), but normalizing by the theoretical upper bound $J_{\max} = \min(|A|,|B|)/\max(|A|,|B|)$, observed overlap reaches only 5--7\% of the achievable range above chance (normalized ES 0.065 helped, 0.047 hurt; per-pair details in Appendix~\ref{app:normalized_es}), confirming that low Jaccard cannot be attributed to set-size constraints.

No surface-level feature (token count, word frequency, syntactic complexity) reliably separates helped from hurt examples. However, pure-model confidence on the target token is strongly predictive within each architecture: hurt examples have lower target-token probability among pure-correct examples ($d = 0.83$--$0.92$), and helped examples have higher probability among pure-wrong examples ($d = 0.73$--$0.77$); see Appendix~\ref{app:confidence_margin}. A Kruskal-Wallis test confirms cross-architecture structure in both hurt ($p = .0005$) and helped ($p = .020$) distributions. The confidence margin thus identifies a per-example predictor of which examples will be affected: attention disrupts marginally correct predictions and rescues near-miss errors (we use `rescue' and `disruption' as descriptive labels for observed transitions, not as causal claims about attention mechanisms), but with architecture-specific thresholds that produce the observed cross-architecture divergence. The rescue pattern generalizes: across all three architectures on LAMBADA, HellaSwag, and ARC-Easy, helped examples have significantly higher pure-model target probability than stayed-wrong examples (9/9 tests, $d = 0.13$--$0.60$; Appendix~\ref{app:cm_cross}). The disruption pattern is weaker on ARC-Easy ($d = 0.13$--$0.26$), absent on HellaSwag under \texttt{acc\_norm}, and absent on binary classification (Appendix~\ref{app:boolq}), likely reflecting structural properties of constrained-choice formats that limit the discriminative power of confidence margins. Full cross-benchmark continuous analysis, including per-architecture effect sizes on multiple-choice formats, appears in Appendix~\ref{app:cm_cross}. This structured per-example disagreement despite similar aggregate performance constitutes predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} arising under architectural variation \citep[cf.][]{damour2022underspecification}.

\paragraph{Multiplicity variation.} Extending the per-example analysis to HellaSwag ($N = 10{,}042$) and PIQA ($N = 1{,}838$) reveals that predictive multiplicity varies systematically across tasks (Table~\ref{tab:multiplicity}). The ambiguity $\alpha$ \citep{marx2020predictive} ranges from 0.14 (PIQA) through 0.15 (HellaSwag) and 0.21 (ARC-Easy) to 0.30 (LAMBADA), forming a task-dependent variation. Evaluation format does not drive multiplicity: HellaSwag (four-way, $\alpha = 0.15$) and PIQA (two-choice, $\alpha = 0.14$) produce nearly identical multiplicity despite different formats; among two-choice benchmarks alone, $\alpha$ spans $3\times$ from 0.14 (PIQA) to 0.41 (WinoGrande; Appendix~\ref{app:winogrande}) to 0.45 (BoolQ; Appendix~\ref{app:boolq}), covering the full six-benchmark range within a single format. Bootstrap 95\% CIs confirm the ordering: all adjacent-benchmark CIs are non-overlapping except PIQA and HellaSwag, whose CIs overlap substantially ($[.127,\,.159]$ vs.\ $[.141,\,.155]$; Appendix, Table~\ref{tab:arceasy_ambiguity}). Moreover, $\alpha$ does not significantly correlate with task accuracy (Spearman $\rho = -0.60$, $p = .24$). Pairwise Jaccard remains uniformly low ($\bar{J} \leq 0.10$, all $p < .01$ across four benchmarks), indicating that architectures affect different examples regardless of how many examples are affected. Within each benchmark, $\alpha$ is stable across attention ratios (variation $< 0.03$), but differs $2\times$ across task types (from reasoning to retrieval), confirming that multiplicity magnitude depends on task, not attention dose \citep[cf.][]{gorbett2026crossmodel}. The near-identical $\alpha$ for PIQA (0.14) and HellaSwag (0.15) despite different evaluation formats suggests a shared reasoning-task floor for multiplicity, while retrieval (LAMBADA, 0.30) and mixed (ARC-Easy, 0.21) tasks produce distinctly higher values.

